  "body": "### The issue\n`PartitionGroup#readyToProcess` formats a not-ready message on every call and `StreamTask` runs it per record. `StreamTask` logs that message at most once every 120s and discards the rest. Tasks that read a busy topic together with a quiet one pay this cost on almost every record.\n\n### Why it happened\nKAFKA-19682 (#20693) replaced parameterized `logger.trace` calls with eager `String.format` calls collected in a `StringBuilder`. `StreamTask#isProcessable` also resolved the message before `maybeLogNotReady` checked the log interval.\n\n### The fix\n- `ReadyToProcessResult` holds a `Supplier<String>`. `maybeLogNotReady` calls it only when it logs.\n- The suppliers capture only immutable locals, so they are safe with `SynchronizedPartitionGroup`.\n- The message for the partition that blocks processing is unchanged. When no partition has data, the message now lists the partitions past their idle deadline. Before, it printed a \"fetched lag\" line for each of them.\n\n### Tests\n- New `PartitionGroupTest#shouldNotFormatNotReadyLogMessageUntilRequested`. It fails on trunk and passes with the fix.\n- `PartitionGroupTest`, `StreamTaskTest` and `SynchronizedPartitionGroupTest` pass unchanged.\n- Microbenchmark with two partitions, one of them idle: 1435 → 85 ns and 3288 → 264 B per call while waiting; 1904 → 214 ns and 1128 → 344 B once the idle deadline has passed.\n\n",
